  "note": "The guitar-advanced week: a mode is drilled by the one note that sets it apart, and that method is the key skill on all seven days in new forms (Dorian's 6th against natural minor, as the 3rd of D7, in three joined positions, Mixolydian's ♭7 by the same method, the two modes one note apart in the same frets, Dorian moved to D, then the jam). Triads on every string set, 9th chords and comping over drums and bass feed the solos, warm-ups climb in choice stages, and each why is a fact the student can build the next drill from.",
  "plan": {
    "title": "Week 1: one note makes the mode",
    "summary": "Find the one note that sets a mode apart, then aim for it: the 6th in Dorian, the ♭7 in Mixolydian, in A and then in D. Triads on every string set and 9th chords give your solos places to land, and each day ends over a band.",
    "progress": "Techniques: string skipping with two and three notes per string up to 120, alternate picking to 124, legato trills with fingers 3-4 and 2-3 up to 104, finger pattern 1-4-2-3. Next: string skips inside scale positions, legato lines across strings.\nExtended chords: Am7, Am9, D9, Gmaj7, Dm9 and G9 in sixteenth strumming up to 100, comping Am9-D9 and the turn to Gmaj7 over drums and bass. Next: 13th chords, shell voicings on a ii–V–I.\nScales: A Dorian in positions 1 to 3 joined on the G string, aiming for the 6th over D7; A Mixolydian aiming for the ♭7 over the G chord; the two compared in the same frets; D Dorian over Dm7-G7; notes named up to fret 15. Next: Phrygian and Lydian by the same method, positions 4 and 5.\nCAGED: Am and D triads on all four string sets, A-G-D triads on three sets, triad notes as targets in a Dorian solo. Next: the inversions of one triad along one string set.",
